For part 1, all borrowed inputs listed—55 and 53 byte split, 84 byte journal header, 40 byte pointer, 64 byte node header, 10 TB pool, weekly scrub schedule, ten-year service life, 1e-9 corruption rate, and 512 byte physical block size—cannot be identified as borrowed solely from the number itself. Each number appears as a standalone value without context indicating its external origin. A reader seeing "55" or "1e-9" would have no way to know these values were imported rather than derived from the repository.

For part 2, nearly every number depends on borrowed inputs. The 108 byte per-unit metadata splits directly on the 55 and 53 byte values. Structural limits like 4040 bytes in a 4 KiB unit and 1944 in a 2 KiB index node depend on the 55 and 53 byte split. Self-witnessing limits (428 bytes in a 512 byte sector, 4012 in 4096 byte) rely on the 84 byte journal header. Tree-height thresholds (865, 1473, 6081, 55233) change if the 40 byte pointer or 64 byte node header changes. The rotating ring's 2048 byte total depends on the 256 byte slot size. The endangered slots count (2 on 512 byte atomic width, 16 on 4096 byte) changes if the 512 byte physical block size or slot size changes. Checksum calculations are highly sensitive: 1.273e12 block reads depend on the 10 TB pool and 4 KiB block size; 1273 corrupt reads depend on the 1e-9 rate; minimum width 31 bits and theoretical miss rates (3906, 244, 15.3 per million at 8, 12, 16 bits) all change if the 1e-9 corruption rate changes.

For part 3, the single piece of metadata that would let a reader immediately verify validity is a Git commit hash attached to each number. For example, "calculated at 7a3f9b2" would let the reader check the repo state at that hash to confirm whether the inputs and calculations remain unchanged. This is more precise than a timestamp since it captures the exact code and data state.

For part 4, the theoretical miss rates (3906, 244, 15.3 per million at 8, 12, 16 bits) are most likely quoted out of context. If cited without the 1e-9 corruption rate assumption or the 10 TB/weekly scrub/ten-year parameters, someone might treat the 15.3 per million figure as a universal guarantee. This could lead to dangerous underestimation of data corruption risks in systems where actual corruption rates exceed 1e-9, potentially causing data integrity failures in production environments.
